  ],
  "text": "For all of history, work meant a person or an animal. Then a machine did it and never got tired. Everything built before this was limited by how many muscles you could afford to feed. Newcomen's engine worked, and it threw away almost all of its heat. It heated the cylinder to fill it with steam, then cooled that same cylinder to collapse it. Every stroke threw away the heat of the stroke before it. Watt's fix was to move the cooling somewhere else. A separate condenser. The cylinder stays hot, the steam collapses next door, and fuel use drops by three quarters. That turned a mine pump into something worth putting in a factory, then a ship, then a train. One modern car engine puts out the sustained work of several hundred horses. Apply that multiplier to every task there is. That is the Industrial Revolution. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
